(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-fairrepresentation"]={"plain":{"bills":{"fairrepresentation":{"slug":"fairrepresentation","title":"The Fair Representation Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-101","heading":"\u00a7 101. The single-member requirement, repealed","part":"TITLE I \u2014 DISTRICTS","body":"**(a) Amendment.** The Act of December 14, 1967 (2 U.S.C. 2c) is amended to read as follows:\n\n> \"In each State entitled in the Ninety-first Congress or in any subsequent Congress thereafter to more than one Representative under an apportionment made pursuant to the provisions of section 2a(a) of this title, there shall be established by law a number of districts ~~equal to the number of Representatives to which such State is so entitled~~ **determined under section 102 of the Fair Representation Act**, and Representatives shall be elected only from districts so established, ~~no district to elect more than one Representative (except that a State which is entitled to more than one Representative and which has in all previous elections elected its Representatives at Large may elect its Representatives at Large to the Ninety-first Congress)~~ **and the number of Representatives elected from each district shall be the number determined under that section**.\"\n\n**(b) Nothing is left to implication.** The repeal of the words struck by subsection (a) **is a determination by Congress that no district is required to elect exactly one Representative**, and no such requirement may be supplied from the history of the provision amended, from any earlier enactment, or from the absence of an express statement.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE I \u2014 DISTRICTS","body":"*Amends the Act of December 14, 1967 (2 U.S.C. 2c), and sections 2a and 5 of title 2, United States Code.*"}]},{"anchor":"sec-102","heading":"\u00a7 102. The number of districts and the number of seats in each","part":"TITLE I \u2014 DISTRICTS","body":"**(a) States entitled to six or more Representatives.** A State entitled to six or more Representatives shall establish districts, and the number of districts and the number of Representatives elected from each **are determined by subsection (c) from the number of Representatives to which the State is entitled.**\n\n**(b) States entitled to five or fewer Representatives.** A State entitled to five or fewer Representatives shall elect them at large, and the State constitutes a single district for every purpose of this Act.\n\n**(c) The determination, which is arithmetic.** The number of Representatives elected from each district is determined as follows, and **a State exercises no choice in the matter** \u2014\n\n**(1)** the State shall establish the **greatest possible number of districts electing five Representatives**, subject to paragraph (2);\n\n**(2)** the Representatives remaining after paragraph (1) is applied shall be elected from districts of **three or of four Representatives**, in the combination that leaves no Representative unassigned and establishes the fewest districts;\n\n**(3)** where more than one combination satisfies paragraphs (1) and (2), the combination establishing the **greater number of districts electing four Representatives** governs; and\n\n**(4)** for purposes of paragraph (1), the greatest possible number of districts electing five Representatives is the greatest number **for which the Representatives remaining can be assigned under paragraph (2)** \u2014 so that where establishing a further district of five would leave a remainder that cannot be composed of districts of three and of four, that further district is not established.\n\n**(c-1) The result is a single answer.** For any number of Representatives, subsection (c) yields **one set of district magnitudes and no other.** The Director of the Census shall publish, within 30 days after the transmission of the statement required by section 2a(a) of title 2, United States Code, the set so determined for each State. **The publication is ministerial, is enforceable by any person, and does not determine the result** \u2014 a State's obligation under subsection (c) does not depend upon it, and where the publication and subsection (c) differ, subsection (c) governs.\n\n**(d) What a State does decide.** A State determines the **boundaries** of its districts, subject to Title IV. **It determines nothing else about their number or size.**\n\n**(e) A district elects the Representatives assigned to it and no others.** Representatives are elected only from districts established under this section, and **a State may not elect some of its Representatives from districts and others at large.**\n\n**(f) A plan that departs from subsection (c) has no effect.** A districting plan under which the number of districts, or the number of Representatives elected from any district, is other than the number subsection (c) determines **has no effect for any election for the office of Representative**, and the State has not established districts conforming to this Title within the meaning of \u00a7 107. **This subsection supplies the consequence by operation of law and requires no act by any person**, and a plan conforming to a determination of a court under \u00a7 106(b) is not a departure from subsection (c)."},{"anchor":"sec-103","heading":"\u00a7 103. Districts of unequal magnitude and the population requirement","part":"TITLE I \u2014 DISTRICTS","body":"**(a) Population per Representative.** Districts within a State shall contain **equal population per Representative**, as nearly as practicable.\n\n**(b) The comparison is per Representative, not per district.** A district electing five Representatives is required to contain approximately five times the population of a district in the same State electing one Representative, and approximately five-thirds the population of a district electing three, **and a plan is not invalid because its districts differ in total population in that proportion.**\n\n**(c) The requirement is satisfied by the quotient.** A plan satisfies subsection (a) where, for every district, the population divided by the number of Representatives elected from it is as nearly equal as practicable to that quotient for every other district in the State. **The total population of a district is not the measure, and no plan may be held invalid on the ground that districts differ in total population where the quotients are as nearly equal as practicable.**"},{"anchor":"sec-104","heading":"\u00a7 104. Conforming amendments","part":"TITLE I \u2014 DISTRICTS","body":"**(a) Election before redistricting.** Section 2a(c) of title 2, United States Code, is amended by striking \"Until a State\" and inserting **\"Except as provided in the Fair Representation Act, until a State\"**.\n\n**(b) Nomination of Representatives elected at large.** Section 5 of title 2, United States Code, is amended by striking \"Candidates for Representative\" and inserting **\"Except as provided in the Fair Representation Act, candidates for Representative\"**.\n\n**(c) The apportionment statutes are not otherwise disturbed.** Nothing in this Act alters the number of Representatives to which any State is entitled, the method by which Representatives are apportioned among the States, or the total number of Representatives."},{"anchor":"sec-105","heading":"\u00a7 105. Winner-take-all prohibited","part":"TITLE I \u2014 DISTRICTS","body":"**(a) Prohibition.** In an election in which more than one Representative is elected from a district, **a method of election under which a group of voters constituting a majority is able to elect every Representative from that district is prohibited**, regardless of form, title, or characterization.\n\n**(b) The methods prohibited include, and are not limited to** \u2014 election of each Representative in a separate contest within the district; election by plurality with each voter casting as many votes as there are seats **and the seats awarded to the candidates receiving the most votes**; election of a slate as a unit; and any method under which the seats are awarded to the candidates or slate receiving the greatest number of votes without regard to the proportion of votes received. **A ballot on which a voter may vote for as many candidates as there are seats under \u00a7 201(b) is not a method prohibited by this section where the seats are allocated among lists under \u00a7 203.**\n\n**(c) The rule stated affirmatively.** Every election in which more than one Representative is elected shall be conducted under Title II, **and where for any reason Title II does not apply, the seats shall be filled by a method under which a group of voters casting a share of the votes at least equal to the quotient of the total votes divided by one more than the number of seats, disregarding any fraction, elects at least one Representative.**\n\n**(d) These conditions apply to any authority invoked to reach the same result.** Where a State reaches, or attempts to reach, an allocation of seats prohibited by subsection (a) under any other authority \u2014 a State constitutional provision, a rule of a political party, a ballot-access requirement, a nomination rule, or any other means \u2014 **the prohibition in subsection (a) applies to that authority.** A State invoking another authority within 180 days after a determination that a method is prohibited by this section shall publish a statement identifying the authority previously relied upon, the authority now invoked, and the difference between them."},{"anchor":"sec-106","heading":"\u00a7 106. A finding of vote dilution is remedied inside the proportional system","part":"TITLE I \u2014 DISTRICTS","body":"**(a) The remedy.** Where a court finds that a districting plan denies or abridges the right to vote on account of race or color, or in contravention of section 4(f)(2) of the Voting Rights Act of 1965, or violates \u00a7 301(b) of the John R. Lewis Voting Rights Act, **the court shall remedy the violation by altering the boundaries of the district or districts concerned, by altering the number of Representatives elected from them, or both.**\n\n**(a-1) How such a violation is established where a district elects more than one Representative.** A claim of vote dilution has been required to be established by showing, among other things, that the group is **sufficiently large and geographically compact to constitute a majority in a single-member district.** That showing is impossible in a district from which more than one Representative is elected, and this Act does not permit such a district to be drawn.\n\n**Accordingly, where a claim concerns a district from which more than one Representative is elected, that showing is not required, and in its place the group shall show that it is sufficiently large and geographically compact to elect a Representative of its choice in a district conforming to \u00a7 102(c), or in a district the court could order under subsection (b).** Every other element of the claim, including political cohesion and the voting patterns of the majority, is unaffected.\n\n**(a-2) The substitution is a determination by Congress.** Subsection (a-1) supplies the showing required in place of one the courts have derived for single-member districts. **The omission of that requirement is a determination by Congress and not a gap**, and no court may treat the impossibility of the substituted showing as a ground for holding that no claim of vote dilution lies as to a district from which more than one Representative is elected.\n\n**(b) The remedial court may exceed the magnitudes fixed by \u00a7 102.** For the purpose of a remedy under subsection (a), a court **may require that a district elect more than five Representatives**, and \u00a7 102(c) does not limit the remedy. The court shall order the smallest departure from \u00a7 102(c) that remedies the violation.\n\n**(c) Single-member districts are not a remedy under this Act.** **No court shall order, and no State shall adopt as a remedy, a district electing one Representative**, except **(1)** in a State entitled to one Representative, or **(2)** where subsection (c-1) applies. **These exceptions are the only exceptions, and the omission of an exception is a determination by Congress and not a gap.** **This subsection states a limitation on the remedy and not on the right**, and it does not limit any relief available under the Voting Rights Act of 1965 other than the configuration of a district.\n\n**(c-1) Relief required by the Constitution is not limited by this section.** **Nothing in this section limits the authority of a court to order any relief the Constitution requires**, and where a court determines that the Constitution requires relief that subsection (c) would otherwise foreclose, **the court shall order that relief and subsection (c) does not apply to it.** Congress states this expressly so that no question arises whether this section purports to confine the remedial authority of a court in a case arising under the Constitution.\n\n**(c-2) The exception is narrow by its terms.** Subsection (c-1) applies only where the relief is **required** by the Constitution, and **not where it is merely one of several forms of relief a court considers adequate.** Where relief conforming to subsection (a) would remedy the violation, that relief is the relief the Constitution permits, and subsection (c-1) does not apply.\n\n**(e) Rule of construction.** Nothing in this section requires or authorizes the use of any racial classification in drawing a district or in setting the number of Representatives elected from one, and **no plan is required by this section to contain any district drawn on the basis of race.**\n\n**(f) The limit of the remedy, stated rather than implied.** Where no configuration of boundaries and magnitudes available under subsection (b) would remedy the violation, **the court shall so find and shall state the configuration that comes nearest**, and shall order that configuration. **A finding under this subsection is a determination that the remedy this Act provides has been exhausted, and it is not a determination that no violation occurred.**"},{"anchor":"sec-107","heading":"\u00a7 107. Where a State has not established districts","part":"TITLE I \u2014 DISTRICTS","body":"**(a) The plan is drawn by a court.** Where a State entitled to six or more Representatives has not established districts conforming to this Title by the date stated in the SCHEDULE, **any voter of the State may bring an action in the district court**, which shall be heard by a court of three judges convened under section 2284 of title 28, United States Code, and the court **shall establish the districts.**\n\n**(b) What governs meanwhile.** Where an election is to be held and no conforming plan has been established by any means, **the Representatives of the State shall be elected at large under Title II** until a conforming plan takes effect. **This subsection supplies the method by operation of law and requires no act by any person.**\n\n**(c) It is not an alternative.** Election at large under subsection (b) does not satisfy the obligation in \u00a7 102, does not extend the date stated in the SCHEDULE, and is not a ground for denying or delaying relief under subsection (a)."},{"anchor":"sec-201","heading":"\u00a7 201. The method","part":"TITLE II \u2014 OPEN-LIST PROPORTIONAL REPRESENTATION","body":"**(a) Requirement.** Each State shall conduct every election in which more than one Representative is elected from a district using the method stated in this Title.\n\n**(b) What the voter does.** A voter may vote for **as many candidates as there are seats** to be filled in the district, or for fewer. **Each vote is for one candidate, and a voter may cast no more than one vote for any candidate.** A voter may vote for candidates standing on different lists, **and may vote for a person whose name does not appear on the ballot by writing in that person's name under subsection (b-1).** **Each vote counts under \u00a7 204 as one full vote for the candidate voted for, and each ballot that casts a vote counts toward the seats the lists win with the same weight as every other such ballot under \u00a7 203(a)(1).**\n\n**(b-1) Votes written in.** A vote cast by writing in the name of a person is a vote under subsection (b) and **counts toward the number of seats under subsection (d).** A name written in for a person whose name does not appear on the ballot **stands as a list under \u00a7 202(c)**, counted as this subsection provides. A vote written in for a person whose name appears on the ballot is a vote for that candidate, on the list on which the candidate stands, and **a ballot indicating a vote for the same candidate more than once, by a mark, by writing in the name, or both, casts one vote for that candidate.** Whether a name written in identifies a person is determined under the standards the State adopts under section 301(a)(6) of the Help America Vote Act of 2002 (52 U.S.C. 21081(a)(6)) and applies to every other contest on the ballot. The chief State election official shall, before the first day on which any ballot may be cast in the election, adopt in writing and publish a standard for the State stating the variations in the form of a name that are disregarded and, where those standards make no provision for it, the rules by which a name written in is matched to a person, and every official who counts ballots in the State shall apply that standard and no other. A standard that leaves the matching to the judgment of the official who counts the ballot, without stating the rule applied, does not satisfy this subsection, and the duty to adopt and publish a standard is ministerial and enforceable by any person. Where a standard is required by this subsection and none satisfying it is published before the first day on which any ballot may be cast in the election, a name written in is matched to a person where, read as the next sentence provides, it identifies one person only. Under any standard, an abbreviation, misspelling, or other minor variation in the form of a name is disregarded, in counting names together under the next sentence without regard to whom either name identifies, and in matching a name to a person where the name so read identifies one person only. **For every count under \u00a7 206, a name written in for a person whose name does not appear on the ballot is counted as written, names that differ only in a variation the preceding sentence disregards being counted together as one name, except that a name differing only in such a variation from two or more names that do not so differ from each other is counted as a name of its own, and each name so counted stands as a list under \u00a7 202(c); which person such a name identifies is determined, under the standard this subsection requires, only where a seat would be filled by that name under \u00a7 204(e).** **A name is written in only on a line the ballot provides under \u00a7 202(a) for writing in a name**, and a line on which more than one person is named casts no vote; **a State may not require that a name so written be accompanied by the marking of an oval, box, or other target**, or by any other mark. On a Federal write-in absentee ballot under section 103 of the Uniformed and Overseas Citizens Absentee Voting Act (52 U.S.C. 20303), a name written in anywhere in the space for the office is a vote under this subsection, and section 103(c)(3) of that Act (52 U.S.C. 20303(c)(3)) also applies.\n\n**(b-2) Nothing is required of a person written in before the election.** A State may not condition the counting of a vote written in on any filing, declaration, or other act by the person written in, by a political party, or by any person other than the voter; the filing deadlines described in \u00a7 205(d) do not apply to a person written in; and a standard under subsection (b-1) may not make the identification of a person depend on any filing or declaration. **A person written in whose name does not appear on the ballot accepts a seat after the election, as \u00a7 204(e) provides.**\n\n**(b-3) A candidate whose name appears on the ballot has already accepted.** The filing or consent by which State law places a person's name on a list, or the petition the person submits under \u00a7 205(c), is that person's acceptance of any seat the person wins, and **a State may not require of a person whose name appears on the ballot any further acceptance as a condition of being elected.**\n\n**(c) No minimum, and no vote for a list alone.** A ballot casting a vote for any number of candidates from one to the number of seats is valid, **and a State may not require a voter to vote for any minimum number of candidates, to vote for the candidates of a single list, to rank any candidate, or to express any preference beyond the votes described in subsection (b).** **A vote may be cast only for a candidate**, and no State may provide for or count a vote for a list alone. **A mark for a political party, including a mark outside the contest for the district, casts no vote for any candidate and is not counted under subsection (d).**\n\n**(d) A ballot indicating votes for more candidates than there are seats.** A ballot on which votes are indicated for more candidates than there are seats, under the standards the State adopts under section 301(a)(6) of the Help America Vote Act of 2002 (52 U.S.C. 21081(a)(6)) and applies to every other contest on the ballot, **casts no vote in the election for the district, for any candidate or any list.** A ballot that a voting system reads as indicating votes for more candidates than there are seats, and on which a name is written in or a target for writing in a name is marked, is not treated as casting no vote until the officials who count the ballots have read the lines for writing in names, and a target so marked with no name written on its line indicates no vote; where, counted under subsection (b-1), the ballot indicates votes for no more candidates than there are seats, it casts those votes, and it is counted in the manner the State counts a ballot the voting system cannot read. The voter shall be notified that the ballot indicates votes for more candidates than there are seats and will cast no vote, and given the opportunity to correct it, as follows \u2014\n\n**(1) Every ballot cast in person is examined before it is cast.** Each State shall ensure that **every ballot cast in person**, at a polling place or at any other place where ballots are cast in person on or before the day of the election, **is examined by equipment at the place where it is cast, before it is cast, for votes for more candidates than there are seats**, and that the notice and the opportunity to correct are given to the voter there. **A State shall not disable that examination, and, except as paragraph (1-A) provides, shall not provide for any ballot to be cast in person without it.** An examination by a person, or by any means other than equipment, does not satisfy this paragraph, which applies notwithstanding section 301(a)(1)(B) and (c)(1) of the Help America Vote Act of 2002 (52 U.S.C. 21081(a)(1)(B), (c)(1)). **For the examination, the equipment treats a mark on a line provided under \u00a7 202(a) for writing in a name as indicating a vote, whether or not a target beside it is marked, and the notice given where the examination so finds states that a name written in may cause the ballot to cast no vote.** A ballot marked by means of a device that does not permit votes for more candidates than there are seats to be indicated has been examined under this paragraph. Where Federal or State law requires a ballot cast in person to be held without being counted until a later time, including a provisional ballot under section 302 of that Act (52 U.S.C. 21082), the examination is made by equipment that does not record the ballot's votes, or by such a device, and is not a counting of the ballot for any purpose of Federal or State law. A ballot the examination finds to indicate votes for more candidates than there are seats is not cast unless the voter, after the notice, directs that it be cast, and no person other than the voter, or a person assisting the voter at the voter's request, may so direct.\n\n**(1-A) Where no equipment is working, voting continues.** Where at a place where ballots are cast in person no equipment or device that makes the examination under paragraph (1) is working, **voting there continues**, and a ballot cast there while none is working is cast without the examination and is treated for every purpose of this subsection as a ballot not cast in person: the notice is given to each voter, in writing, as paragraph (2) provides, before the ballot is cast. The officials conducting the election at that place shall restore the examination as soon as practicable, and **the State shall publish under \u00a7 206(b), for each such place, the times at which no such equipment or device was working and the number of ballots cast there in that time.** No person may delay or stop voting at a place on the ground that the examination is not available there. **This paragraph keeps voting going and excuses nothing else. Where no equipment or device is working at a place because the State, or a person acting for it, disabled it or did not provide, program, or maintain it, the State has violated paragraph (1), and \u00a7 505 applies; equipment is not working only where it cannot make the examination, and the officials at the place shall record the time each failure began and ended.**\n\n**(2) Every other ballot.** For a ballot not cast in person, the notice is given by the voter education and instructions described in section 301(a)(1)(B) of the Help America Vote Act of 2002 (52 U.S.C. 21081(a)(1)(B)), and by instructions accompanying the ballot.\n\n**(3) The notice under that Act.** **In an election under this Title, the notice required by section 301(a)(1)(A)(iii) of that Act is notice that the ballot indicates votes for more candidates than there are seats**, and a ballot indicating votes for more than one candidate but not for more candidates than there are seats does not require it. The voter education described in section 301(a)(1)(B)(i) of that Act notifies each voter, in an election under this Title, of the effect of voting for more candidates than there are seats, and not that voting for more than one candidate causes the ballot to cast no vote.\n\n**(4) The Federal write-in absentee ballot.** For an election under this Title, the Presidential designee under the Uniformed and Overseas Citizens Absentee Voting Act shall provide, in the Federal write-in absentee ballot prescribed under section 103 of that Act (52 U.S.C. 20303) and in the instructions for it, a means of writing in the names of as many persons as there are seats, the statement described in \u00a7 202(d), and the notice described in this subsection. The designee shall first do so not later than one year before the first election under this Title.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE II \u2014 OPEN-LIST PROPORTIONAL REPRESENTATION","body":"*Enacted under Article I, section 4. **This Title stands on its own and amends no existing statute.***"}]},{"anchor":"sec-202","heading":"\u00a7 202. The ballot","part":"TITLE II \u2014 OPEN-LIST PROPORTIONAL REPRESENTATION","body":"**(a) Form.** The ballot shall display each list, the name of each candidate standing on it, a means of voting for each candidate, **and, for each seat, a separate line on which the voter may write in the name of one person.** **The ballot provides no means of voting for a list alone.**\n\n**(b) Order within a list.** Candidates within a list appear in the order the list states. Except as \u00a7 204(d) provides, **the order in which candidates appear does not affect which of them are elected**, which is determined by \u00a7 204.\n\n**(c) Independent candidates.** A candidate who stands on no list appears on the ballot **as a list bearing that candidate's name and containing that candidate alone.** Every provision of this Title applies to such a list.\n\n**(d) A statement on the ballot.** The ballot shall state, in a form the State prescribes: **the number of candidates the voter may vote for, which is the number of seats; that the voter may vote for candidates on one list or on several, may write in the name of a person whose name does not appear on the ballot, one name on each line provided, who takes a seat won only by accepting it after the election, and may vote for no candidate more than once; that every ballot counts equally toward the number of seats the lists win, its weight divided among the lists in proportion to its votes for the candidates of each, so that a ballot casting one vote fewer than the number of seats for candidates of one list and one vote for a candidate of another gives the first that number of votes divided by the number of seats, and the second one divided by the number of seats, stated in the figures for the district (in a district electing five Representatives, four votes and one, four-fifths and one-fifth); that the seats a list wins are taken by its candidates with the most votes, each vote counting as one full vote for the candidate voted for, whatever other candidates the ballot votes for; and that a ballot indicating votes for more candidates than there are seats casts no vote.**"},{"anchor":"sec-203","heading":"\u00a7 203. Allocation of seats among lists","part":"TITLE II \u2014 OPEN-LIST PROPORTIONAL REPRESENTATION","body":"**(a) The computation.** The seats in a district are allocated among the lists as follows, and **the computation admits of no discretion** \u2014\n\n**(1)** each ballot that casts a vote for at least one candidate carries **a weight of one, divided among the lists on which the candidates it votes for stand in proportion to the number of its votes for the candidates of each list**, so that a list receives from the ballot the number of the ballot's votes for candidates on that list divided by the number of all its votes; and the **list total** of each list is the sum, over every pair of whole numbers V and k with V at least one and k not greater than V, of the number of ballots casting V votes in all, k of them for candidates on that list, multiplied by k and divided by V \u2014 **computed and compared as an exact fraction, without rounding**;\n\n**(2)** for each list, a quotient is computed by dividing the list total by 1, by 2, by 3, and by each succeeding whole number, until the number of quotients computed for that list equals the number of seats to be filled, each quotient being computed and compared as an exact fraction, without rounding;\n\n**(3)** the quotients so computed for all lists are arranged in descending order, and **a seat is awarded for each of the highest quotients, in that order, until every seat is filled**; and\n\n**(4)** the number of seats a list wins is the number of its quotients that were awarded a seat.\n\n**(b) No threshold.** **No list is required to receive any minimum share of the votes to be awarded a seat**, and no State may impose such a requirement. The number of seats in the district is the only threshold, and it is not stated as one.\n\n**(c) A list that wins more seats than it has candidates.** Where a list is awarded more seats than the number of candidates standing on it, **the excess seats are awarded by continuing the order in subsection (a)(3) among the quotients of the remaining lists**, disregarding every further quotient of the list that has run out of candidates.\n\n**(d) Ties.** Where two quotients are equal and the seats remaining are fewer than the lists holding them, the seat is awarded to the list with the greater list total; where the list totals are also equal, **the tie is resolved by lot**, and the result is recorded and reused in any recount. The lot is drawn in public by the chief State election official, after notice of its time and place, published and given to each list or candidate concerned as soon as the tie is known, in time for its result to be published within the period stated in the SCHEDULE, and the duty is ministerial and enforceable by any person."},{"anchor":"sec-204","heading":"\u00a7 204. Which candidates take a list's seats","part":"TITLE II \u2014 OPEN-LIST PROPORTIONAL REPRESENTATION","body":"**(a) The order is the voters' order.** The seats a list wins are filled by its candidates **in descending order of their candidate totals under subsection (b).**\n\n**(b) Each vote counts as one full vote for the candidate voted for.** The **candidate total** of each candidate is **the number of votes cast for that candidate**, and each vote counts as one, whatever the number of votes the ballot casts and whatever lists its other votes are for. A ballot counts toward list totals only as \u00a7 203(a)(1) provides, and **the division of a ballot's weight among lists under \u00a7 203(a)(1) does not reduce any vote counted under this subsection.**\n\n**(c) Ties.** Where candidates of the same list have equal candidate totals and the seats the list wins would be filled by some but not all of them, the tie is resolved by lot, and the result is recorded and reused in any recount. The lot is drawn in public by the chief State election official, after notice of its time and place, published and given to each list or candidate concerned as soon as the tie is known, in time for its result to be published within the period stated in the SCHEDULE, and the duty is ministerial and enforceable by any person.\n\n**(d) The list's stated order governs only where no candidate on the list has received a vote.** Where no vote has been cast for any candidate on a list, the seats it wins are filled in the order the list states.\n\n**(e) A person written in accepts the seat after the election.** Where a seat would be filled under this section by a person whose name does not appear on the ballot and who is written in under \u00a7 201(b-1), the chief State election official shall notify that person not later than the day after the State determines the seats each list wins, or the last day State law fixes for certifying the result, whichever is earlier; a notice not given by that day is received, for purposes of the SCHEDULE, on the day the State publishes under \u00a7 206(b) the order in which seats were awarded; an acceptance filed after the polls close and before notice is received is filed within the period; and **where the person has not filed with the chief State election official, within the period stated in the SCHEDULE, a written statement signed by the person that the person accepts the seat, or where under \u00a7 201(b-1) the name identifies no one person, the seat is awarded under \u00a7 203(c) as though that person's list had no candidate remaining.** The chief State election official determines, under the standard \u00a7 201(b-1) requires, which person a name by which a seat would be filled under this section identifies, or that it identifies no one person, and publishes the determination by the day notice is due. Where a seat passes under this subsection to another person to whom this subsection applies, or a recount under \u00a7 206(b-1) first gives a seat to such a person, the notice to that person is due the day after the publication this subsection or \u00a7 206(b-1) requires, and a notice not given by that day is received on that publication. A State may require nothing in the statement beyond the acceptance and the signature, and may not refuse a statement on any ground relating to the person's qualifications for the office, which the House of Representatives judges under article I, section 5 of the Constitution. A signature in any form, including an electronic signature, signs the statement, and a State may not require that it be in ink, witnessed, or notarized. The chief State election official shall publish, not later than the day after the period ends, whether the statement was filed and, where it was not, the list to which the seat is awarded; the duties of notice and publication are ministerial and enforceable by any person.\n\n**(e-1) No person written in is elected before accepting.** A State shall not certify the election of, or issue a certificate of election to, a person to whom subsection (e) applies until that person has filed the statement it describes. A person who has not filed it within the period stated in the SCHEDULE has not been elected, and the seat is filled, as part of the election, as subsection (e) provides. **The State shall certify the result for every other seat in the district within the time State law fixes, and for the seat to which subsection (e) applies not later than the second day after the later of (A) the day the chief State election official receives the statement from the last person to whom subsection (e) applies as to that seat, or the day the period stated in the SCHEDULE for that person ends, whichever is earlier, or, where the seat passes because a name identifies no one person and no further person to whom subsection (e) applies is reached, the day that determination is published, and (B) the day any recount determines the seats each list wins, whatever day State law fixes for certifying the result.**\n\n**(e-2) The award is made at the election.** For purposes of sections 7 and 8 of title 2, United States Code, the election of a person who files the statement subsection (e) describes, and an award of a seat under subsection (e), shall each be treated as made at the election held on the day section 7 establishes, **and a seat so filled or awarded is not vacant by reason of a failure to elect.**"},{"anchor":"sec-205","heading":"\u00a7 205. Access to a list, and access to the ballot","part":"TITLE II \u2014 OPEN-LIST PROPORTIONAL REPRESENTATION","body":"**(b) Nomination by a party.** A political party may state the candidates standing on its list, in the manner provided by State law.\n\n**(c) Access by petition, which no party may withhold.** A person who will meet, when elected, the qualification of inhabitancy stated in article I, section 2, clause 2 of the Constitution and, when the person takes the oath of office, the qualifications of age and citizenship stated there, and who submits a petition signed by the number of voters of the district stated in the SCHEDULE **shall appear on the ballot**, either on a list the person names and which consents, or as a list of that person alone under \u00a7 202(c). **No signature requirement under this subsection may exceed the number stated in the SCHEDULE**, and a State may not condition ballot access under this subsection on the consent, endorsement, or non-objection of any political party.\n\n**(d) A State may require of a list what it requires of every list.** A State may impose filing deadlines, filing fees within the limit stated in the SCHEDULE, signature verification, and a requirement that a candidate will meet those qualifications at those times, **provided that each applies to every list and to every candidate on the same terms.**\n\n**(d-1) Verification of signatures.** Because the number of valid signatures is determined by the officials who verify them, **a signature submitted under subsection (c) may be rejected only on a ground stated in writing at the time of rejection**, and the State shall \u2014\n\n**(1)** notify the person who submitted the petition of each signature rejected and the ground for its rejection, within the period stated in the SCHEDULE; and\n\n**(2)** afford a period stated in the SCHEDULE within which further signatures may be submitted, or a rejected signature shown to be valid.\n\n**(d-2) A rejection not stated is no rejection.** A ground is stated only where the writing identifies the requirement of State law the signature fails to meet and the fact on which the failure rests, and a statement that a signature is invalid or not verified, without more, states no ground. A signature rejected without a written ground, or rejected without notice given under subsection (d-1)(1) within the period stated in the SCHEDULE, **counts as valid**, and a petition is sufficient where the signatures accepted, together with those that count as valid under this subsection, meet the requirement of subsection (c). **This subsection supplies the consequence by operation of law and requires no act by any person.**\n\n**(e) These and only these.** The conditions a State may impose on access to the ballot under this Title are those stated in subsections (c) and (d) and no others. **The omission of a condition is a determination by Congress and not a gap.**\n\n**(f) The number of candidates on a list.** A list may contain any number of candidates not exceeding the number of Representatives to be elected from the district. **A State may not require that a list contain any minimum number of candidates**, and \u00a7 203(c) supplies the consequence where a list wins more seats than it has candidates."},{"anchor":"sec-206","heading":"\u00a7 206. Tabulation and publication","part":"TITLE II \u2014 OPEN-LIST PROPORTIONAL REPRESENTATION","body":"**(a) Tabulation is completed where the ballots are cast.** **Each State shall ensure that** the votes cast for each candidate, including each person written in, in total and on ballots that also vote for a candidate of another list, and for each list the number of ballots for each pair of numbers V and k described in \u00a7 203(a)(1), **are** counted, and the count published, **at the precinct or other place where the ballots are cast**, before the ballots or the counts are transmitted elsewhere. **The duty is performed by the officials who conduct the count at that place**, and a State may not require that ballots be transmitted elsewhere before the count at that place is completed and published. For a ballot not cast in person, the place where the ballot is cast is the place where it is first counted, and the duty is performed by the officials who count it there. **A ballot cast in person that Federal or State law requires to be held without being counted because the voter's eligibility to vote in the election has not been determined is counted, and the count published, as a ballot not cast in person is**; every other ballot cast in person is counted under the first sentence of this subsection, whatever law requires it to be held. This subsection does not apply to a provisional ballot under section 302 of the Help America Vote Act of 2002 (52 U.S.C. 21082), which is counted as that section provides and is included in the publication under subsection (b).\n\n**(b) Publication.** Each State shall publish, in a machine-readable form and within the period stated in the SCHEDULE: the votes cast for each candidate in each precinct, including each person written in; for each list in each precinct, the number of ballots for each pair of numbers V and k described in \u00a7 203(a)(1); for each candidate and each list, those numbers summed over the district; for each candidate, the votes cast on ballots that also vote for a candidate of another list, in each precinct and in the district; the number of ballots cast in each precinct, and the number of them that cast no vote under \u00a7 201(d); for each place where ballots are cast in person, the times and numbers of ballots stated in \u00a7 201(d)(1-A); the list totals under \u00a7 203(a)(1) and every quotient computed under \u00a7 203(a)(2), each stated as an exact fraction, and the candidate totals under \u00a7 204(b); the result of each lot drawn under \u00a7 203(d) or \u00a7 204(c); and the order in which seats were awarded.\n\n**(b-1) Recounts.** A recount determines the counts and totals subsection (b) requires, and the State publishes them under that subsection within the period stated in the SCHEDULE for a recount.\n\n**(c) The duty is ministerial** and is enforceable by any person, and a person denied information this section requires to be published is aggrieved within the meaning of \u00a7 505(a-1).\n\n**(d) The computation is reproducible.** Because the allocation under \u00a7 203 and the order under \u00a7 204 are determined entirely by the published totals, the published results of any lot, and the publications under \u00a7 204(e), **any person may recompute them**, and a discrepancy between the published allocation or the candidates declared elected and those the published totals, results, and publications produce is a violation of this Title."},{"anchor":"sec-207","heading":"\u00a7 207. Definitions","part":"TITLE II \u2014 OPEN-LIST PROPORTIONAL REPRESENTATION","body":"In this Title \u2014\n\n**(1)** the term **\"list\"** means the candidates who stand together for election in a district, whether nominated by a political party, standing alone under \u00a7 202(c), or written in under \u00a7 201(b-1);\n\n**(2)** the terms **\"list total\"** and **\"candidate total\"** have the meanings given in \u00a7 203(a)(1) and \u00a7 204(b), respectively;\n\n**(3)** the term **\"quotient\"** means a number computed under \u00a7 203(a)(2);\n\n**(4)** the term **\"seat\"** means an office of Representative to be filled in the district;\n\n**(5)** the term **\"vote\"** means a vote cast for a candidate under \u00a7 201(b), including a vote written in under \u00a7 201(b-1), and no other mark on a ballot is a vote for any purpose of this Title;\n\n**(6)** the term **\"candidate\"** includes a person written in under \u00a7 201(b-1); and\n\n**(7)** a ballot is **\"cast in person\"** where the voter marks it at the place where it is delivered to an election official or to a voting system, whatever its designation under State law, including a ballot designated absentee, early, provisional, or emergency, and a ballot marked elsewhere and delivered to that place is not cast in person."},{"anchor":"sec-208","heading":"\u00a7 208. Payments to States","part":"TITLE II \u2014 OPEN-LIST PROPORTIONAL REPRESENTATION","body":"**(a) Payment.** The Election Assistance Commission shall pay to each State the amount stated in the SCHEDULE for each individual registered to vote in elections for Federal office in the State, to be used to carry out this Act, including the acquisition and programming of equipment, ballot design and printing, training, tabulation, publication under \u00a7 206, audits, and voter education. **No action of the Commission under this Act is an action requiring approval under section 20928 of title 52, United States Code. The determination of the amount owed to a State, and each payment and reimbursement under this section, is a ministerial act and is made by the Executive Director of the Commission or any individual serving as acting Executive Director; for any period in which no individual holds or acts in that office, by the Secretary of the Treasury; and, where any part of the amount owed on a claim remains unpaid on the 91st day after the claim is submitted, also by the Secretary of the Treasury, who shall pay the amount unpaid with the interest subsection (b) provides.** The amounts appropriated by subsection (b) are available to the Secretary, without transfer, for each payment, payment of interest, and reimbursement the Secretary makes under this section. A payment by any individual authorized to make it discharges the amount owed to the extent of the payment, and no individual may certify a voucher for a payment on a claim of more than the amount owed on it, with interest under subsection (b), less every amount already certified for payment on that claim. The individual who makes a payment or reimbursement under this section certifies the voucher for it under section 3325(a)(1) of title 31, United States Code, as the head of the executive agency concerned.\n\n**(b) Appropriation.** There are appropriated to the Election Assistance Commission, **out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated and without fiscal year limitation**, such sums as are necessary for payments under subsection (a), for interest under this subsection, and for reimbursement under this subsection. **Amounts appropriated by this subsection are available on the date of enactment and remain available until expended. The appropriation is not subject to apportionment delay, reserve, or programmatic withholding and is available notwithstanding sections 1512 and 1513 of title 31, United States Code; an obligation or expenditure of it in the amounts subsection (a) and this subsection require does not violate section 1517(a) of that title by reason of an apportionment, or of an amount permitted by regulations prescribed under section 1514(a) of that title, that makes available less; and a failure to request, apportion, allot, obligate, or disburse the amounts appropriated does not render them unavailable**. Section 1012 of the Impoundment Control Act of 1974 (2 U.S.C. 683) does not apply to the amounts appropriated by this subsection, and section 1013(b) of that Act (2 U.S.C. 684(b)) permits no deferral of them; for purposes of sections 1015 and 1016 of that Act (2 U.S.C. 686, 687), those amounts are budget authority required under that Act to be made available for obligation. A claim is submitted when it is delivered to the Commission or to the Secretary of the Treasury and states the number of individuals registered to vote in elections for Federal office in the State and the amount claimed; the Commission may prescribe a form, and a claim stating those matters is not incomplete for want of it. The Commission and the Secretary shall each publish an electronic address for the purpose; a claim sent electronically to such an address is delivered when sent, and a claim sent by mail to the principal office of either is delivered not later than the third day after mailing. A claim delivered to both is one claim, submitted on the earlier delivery, and the Secretary shall forward to the Commission a copy of each claim delivered to the Secretary. Where a State has submitted a claim and the amount owed has not been paid in full within 90 days after its submission, **the amount unpaid bears interest at the rate determined under section 1961(a) of title 28, United States Code, for the calendar week preceding the 91st day, computed daily and compounded annually, from the 91st day until it is paid, whether or not a court so orders**, and the State may recover the amount owed and the interest in an action in the United States Court of Federal Claims. A judgment in such an action is paid under section 1304 of title 31, United States Code, as a judgment for which payment is not otherwise provided, with interest under this subsection to the date of payment notwithstanding section 1304(b) of that title, and the Commission shall reimburse the amount paid from the amounts appropriated by this subsection. A limitation in a later Act on the use of the amounts appropriated by this subsection does not reduce the amount owed to a State or the interest on it unless that Act refers to this subsection expressly.\n\n**(c) No requirement of this Act is conditioned on the payment.** **The obligations imposed by this Act take effect whether or not a payment under this section is made**, and a failure to pay is not a defense to any requirement of this Act, a ground for delaying an election, or a ground for conducting an election by any other method. **The method by which Representatives are elected does not depend upon an appropriation, and no Act making appropriations is to be construed to suspend it unless that Act refers to this subsection expressly.**\n\n**(d) The Commission administers the payment and does not govern the method of election.** The individual who makes a payment under subsection (a) determines the amount owed to a State by applying the SCHEDULE to the number of registered voters, **and neither the Election Assistance Commission nor that individual exercises any authority over the method by which a State conducts an election under this Act.** Neither the Commission nor any individual who makes or is authorized to make a payment under this section may condition, reduce, delay or withhold a payment on a State's conduct of an election, and **a determination under this section has no effect on any obligation imposed by this Act.** Nothing in this subsection limits the functions of the Commission under sections 20962 and 20971 of title 52, United States Code, including the adoption of voluntary voting system guidelines for, and the testing and certification of, voting systems that carry out \u00a7 201(d)(1), \u00a7 203, \u00a7 204, and \u00a7 206, and neither the adoption of such a guideline nor such a certification is an exercise of authority over the method by which a State conducts an election.\n\n**(e) Enforcement of this Act does not run through the Help America Vote Act.** The requirements of this Title are enforceable under \u00a7 505, **and the omission of an amendment to the enforcement provisions of the Help America Vote Act of 2002 is a determination by Congress and not a gap.**"},{"anchor":"sec-301","heading":"\u00a7 301. More than one party may nominate the same candidate","part":"TITLE III \u2014 FUSION IN SINGLE-WINNER FEDERAL ELECTIONS","body":"**(a) Requirement.** In an election for the office of Senator, or for the office of Representative in a State entitled to one Representative, **a State shall permit a candidate to be nominated by more than one political party**, and shall permit each nominating party's nomination to appear on the ballot.\n\n**(b) Consent.** A nomination is effective only with the candidate's written consent, which the candidate may withhold as to any party.\n\n**(c) Ballot form.** The candidate's name shall appear on the ballot **once for each nominating party**, identified with that party, or in such other form as the State prescribes which permits a voter to cast a vote identified with a particular nominating party.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE III \u2014 FUSION IN SINGLE-WINNER FEDERAL ELECTIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-302","heading":"\u00a7 302. The votes are added together","part":"TITLE III \u2014 FUSION IN SINGLE-WINNER FEDERAL ELECTIONS","body":"**(a) Aggregation.** The votes cast for a candidate on every party line are **added together**, and the total is the candidate's vote in the election.\n\n**(b) Publication by line.** The State shall publish the votes cast on each party line separately, **and the separate figures have no effect on who is elected.**\n\n**(c) No party is disadvantaged by the arithmetic.** Where State law confers a benefit or imposes a requirement measured by the votes a party received \u2014 ballot access, party qualification, the order of parties on a ballot, or any similar consequence \u2014 **the measure is the votes cast on that party's line**, and a State may not attribute the whole of a fusion candidate's vote to one nominating party or divide it among them by any rule other than the votes actually cast on each line."},{"anchor":"sec-303","heading":"\u00a7 303. What a State may still require, and what it may not","part":"TITLE III \u2014 FUSION IN SINGLE-WINNER FEDERAL ELECTIONS","body":"**(a) Preserved.** A State may require that a party nominating a candidate satisfy the conditions it applies to every party, may set filing deadlines applicable to all nominations equally, and may require that the candidate be qualified for the office.\n\n**(b) Prohibited.** A State may not refuse to print a nomination on the ground that another party has nominated the same candidate; may not require a party to nominate a candidate no other party has nominated; may not treat a fusion nomination as a withdrawal of any nomination; and **may not condition any nomination on the consent of another party.**\n\n**(c) These conditions apply to any authority invoked to reach the same result.** Where a State reaches, or attempts to reach, the exclusion of a fusion nomination under any other authority, including a rule of a political party given the force of State law, **the prohibitions in subsection (b) apply to that authority**, and \u00a7 105(d) applies to the statement required upon invoking it.\n\n**(d) These and only these.** The grounds on which a State may refuse a nomination under this Title are those stated in subsection (a) and no others, and **the omission of a ground is a determination by Congress and not a gap.**"},{"anchor":"sec-401","heading":"\u00a7 401. The criteria list is opened, and only for this","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 DISTRICTING CRITERIA","body":"**(a) Amendment.** Section 301(a) of the John R. Lewis Voting Rights Act is amended to read as follows:\n\n> \"**(a) Duty.** A congressional district shall be drawn on the following criteria and no others \u2014 population equality **per Representative elected from the district**; contiguity; compactness; respect for the boundaries of political subdivisions and of communities of interest; **the number of Representatives elected from the district, as determined by \u00a7 102 of the Fair Representation Act**; and compliance with the Voting Rights Act.\"\n\n**(b) Nothing else is admitted.** The amendment made by subsection (a) **admits the criterion it names and no other**, and the closed character of the list stated in that subsection is unaffected.\n\n**(c) Where that Act is not in effect.** Where the John R. Lewis Voting Rights Act is not in effect, **the criteria set out in the text quoted in subsection (a) apply of their own force to a congressional districting plan**, and are enforceable under \u00a7 505 of this Act. **This Act is independently operative**, and no requirement of this Act depends upon the enactment or continued force of any other Act.","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE IV \u2014 DISTRICTING CRITERIA","body":"*Amends the John R. Lewis Voting Rights Act.*"}]},{"anchor":"sec-402","heading":"\u00a7 402. Mid-decade redistricting","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 DISTRICTING CRITERIA","body":"**(a) Prohibition.** A State that has established districts following an apportionment **may not establish different districts until after the next apportionment.**\n\n**(b) Exceptions, and these only.** Subsection (a) does not apply to districts established to comply with an order of a court, with the Constitution, with the Voting Rights Act of 1965, with the John R. Lewis Voting Rights Act, or with a requirement of this Act. **A plan adopted under this subsection shall depart from the plan it replaces no further than the compliance requires.**\n\n**(c) Consequence.** A plan established in violation of subsection (a) **has no effect**, and the plan previously in force governs the election."},{"anchor":"sec-403","heading":"\u00a7 403. The record","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 DISTRICTING CRITERIA","body":"**(a) Publication.** The authority establishing a congressional districting plan shall publish, with the plan and before its first use: the number of Representatives elected from each district and the arithmetic under \u00a7 102(c) producing it; the criteria applied; the data used, by source and type; each alternative plan considered; and the roll-call vote of each member of the adopting body.\n\n**(b) The duty is ministerial** and enforceable by any person.\n\n**(b-1) What fails to satisfy subsection (a).** A publication does not satisfy subsection (a) where it states a criterion without stating how it was applied, states that data were used without identifying the source and type, or states that alternatives were considered without identifying them. **A statement that the plan complies with this Act, without more, does not satisfy any requirement of subsection (a).**\n\n**(b-2) Consequence.** In an action under \u00a7 505, **the State may not rely on a criterion, datum, or consideration absent from the published record** to explain or defend a districting plan.\n\n**(c) Relation to the John R. Lewis Voting Rights Act.** Where \u00a7 301B of that Act applies, this section is **cumulative of it** and does not displace it, and a publication satisfying that section and stating the matters in subsection (a) satisfies both."},{"anchor":"sec-404","heading":"\u00a7 404. The action and the remedy under that Act, where districts elect more than one Representative","part":"TITLE IV \u2014 DISTRICTING CRITERIA","body":"**(a) Amendment.** Section 302(b) of the John R. Lewis Voting Rights Act is amended by adding at the end the following sentence:\n\n> \"**Where the Fair Representation Act applies to the State, a conforming plan is one that conforms to that Act, and a plan the court adopts is adopted under \u00a7 106 and \u00a7 107 of that Act.**\"\n\n**(b) The legislature draws boundaries and does not choose magnitudes.** A plan enacted by a legislature under section 302(b) of that Act conforms to \u00a7 102(c) of this Act, **except where the court has found under \u00a7 106(b) that no plan conforming to \u00a7 102(c) remedies the violation and has stated the number of Representatives a district is to elect**, in which case the legislature may establish boundaries for districts electing the numbers the court has stated and may alter nothing else.\n\n**(c) How the divergence standard applies to a proportional plan.** For purposes of section 301(d) of that Act, **a divergence between the share of seats and the share of votes that would be produced by any plan conforming to \u00a7 102(c) of this Act and \u00a7 203 of this Act is explained by the criteria in section 301(a) of that Act**, as amended by \u00a7 401. A divergence exceeding that is not so explained, and the comparison under section 301(d-1) of that Act is to plans establishing the districts \u00a7 102(c) requires with different boundaries.\n\n**(d) One court.** Where an action under section 302 of that Act and an action under \u00a7 505 of this Act challenge the same districting plan, **they shall be heard together by one court of three judges** convened under section 2284 of title 28, United States Code, and a district court of the United States in which the later action is filed shall transfer it to the court in which the earlier is pending. This subsection does not apply to an action brought in a State court under \u00a7 505(g).\n\n**(e) Where that Act is not in effect.** Where the John R. Lewis Voting Rights Act is not in effect, this section has no application, **and no requirement of this Act depends upon it.**"},{"anchor":"sec-501","heading":"\u00a7 501. Effective dates","part":"TITLE V \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Titles I, II and IV.** Titles I, II and IV, and the amendments made by them, apply to elections for the office of Representative held pursuant to the apportionment resulting from the decennial census conducted in 2030 and to each subsequent election.\n\n**(b) Title III.** Title III applies to elections for the office of Senator, and for the office of Representative in a State entitled to one Representative, held in 2032 and in each subsequent year.\n\n**(c) Section 208.** Section 208 takes effect on the date of enactment.\n\n**(d) No election is delayed.** **No provision of this Act delays, postpones or conditions the holding of any election on its date fixed by law.**","partHeads":[{"heading":"TITLE V \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-502","heading":"\u00a7 502. Severability","part":"TITLE V \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"Each Title is severable from each other Title and each section from each other section. **Congress would have enacted each Title of this Act independently of the others.**\n\n**In particular** \u2014 **(1)** Title III is independent of Titles I, II and IV, and were they held invalid in whole, Congress would have enacted Title III alone; **(2)** Titles I and II are independent of Title III and of Title IV; **(3)** were \u00a7 102(c) held invalid, \u00a7 102(a), (b), (d) and (e) remain in force, **and a State shall establish districts each electing not fewer than three and not more than five Representatives**, which a court shall determine in an action under \u00a7 505 where the State does not; **(4)** were \u00a7 106(b) or \u00a7 106(c) held invalid, the remainder of \u00a7 106 remains in force; **(5)** were Title I held invalid in whole, the criterion inserted in section 301(a) of the John R. Lewis Voting Rights Act by \u00a7 401(a) referring to \u00a7 102 of this Act has no application, and the remainder of that section as amended remains in force; and **(6)** were the award of a seat to another list under \u00a7 204(e) held invalid, a seat a person written in does not accept within the period stated in the SCHEDULE, or that a name identifying no one person under \u00a7 201(b-1) would fill, is vacant and is filled as article I, section 2, clause 4 of the Constitution provides, \u00a7 204(e-2) and the words of \u00a7 204(e-1) stating that the seat is filled as part of the election have no effect as to that seat, and the remainder of \u00a7 204(e) and (e-1) remains in force; and were the requirement that a person written in accept a seat held invalid, the person whom a name by which a seat would be filled identifies under \u00a7 201(b-1) is elected without filing the statement \u00a7 204(e) describes, and the words of \u00a7 202(d) stating that such a person takes a seat only by accepting it have no effect."},{"anchor":"sec-503","heading":"\u00a7 503. Application, and the authority for each application","part":"TITLE V \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Elections for Federal office.** Every requirement of this Act applies to an election for Federal office, and **as to those elections this Act is enacted under the authority of Congress to make or alter regulations respecting the times, places and manner of holding elections for Senators and Representatives.**\n\n**(b) No effect on elections for State or local office.** Nothing in this Act applies to an election for State or local office, or to the districts used in such an election. **A State remains free to elect its own officers by any method it chooses**, and no provision of this Act may be construed to require, forbid or influence its choice.\n\n**(c) Districts drawn for both purposes.** Where a State uses the same districts for congressional and for State legislative elections, **this Act governs only their use in elections for Federal office**, and a State may draw separate districts for its own elections.\n\n**(d) No narrowing by implication.** The statement of one authority for a provision **is not a determination that no other authority supports it.**\n\n**(e) The displacement of State law is intended and is stated in the terms the canon requires.** Congress is aware that the manner of electing Representatives has long been regulated by the States, and **intends by this Act to displace State law on every matter this Act addresses.** **No presumption against the displacement of State law, and no canon requiring a clear statement before a federal statute alters a traditional function of the States, applies to any provision of this Act**, each such presumption being displaced by this subsection. **Where a provision of State law is inconsistent with this Act, this Act governs.**\n\n**(f) This Act regulates the conduct of federal elections and does not commandeer.** The requirements of this Act are imposed under the authority of Congress to make regulations respecting the manner of holding elections for Senators and Representatives, **which is an authority to regulate the conduct of those elections directly.** A State that does not establish districts is answered by \u00a7 107(b), under which the method this Act prescribes applies of its own force. **The duties this Act places on a State and on the officers who conduct elections for Senator or Representative, including those in \u00a7 201(d)(1) and \u00a7 206(a), are regulations of the manner of holding those elections, made under the authority of Congress under Article I, section 4 to make such regulations and to require their performance by the officers who conduct those elections.**"},{"anchor":"sec-504","heading":"\u00a7 504. Defenses and construction","part":"TITLE V \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Defenses exclusive.** The defenses, immunities, exceptions and limitations available in an action under this Act are those and only those expressly stated in it. **No defense is to be inferred from the subject matter, from the identity of the defendant, or from the absence of an express statement.**\n\n**(b) No reconstitution.** A limitation omitted from this Act may not be reintroduced as an element of a plaintiff's case, a pleading requirement, a standard of proof, or a prudential limitation on jurisdiction.\n\n**(c) Terms defined because they would otherwise be construed.** In this Act \u2014\n\n**(1)** **\"district\"** means a geographic area from which one or more Representatives are elected;\n\n**(2)** **\"establish,\"** with respect to districts, means to fix their boundaries and to make them applicable to an election, by whatever instrument and by whatever body;\n\n**(3)** **\"State\"** includes the District of Columbia, the Commonwealth of Puerto Rico, and any other territory or possession of the United States, as to the election of a Delegate or Resident Commissioner, to the extent the office is filled by election;\n\n**(4)** **\"Representative\"** includes a Delegate and a Resident Commissioner to the Congress, except that **a State entitled to elect only a Delegate or a Resident Commissioner is a State entitled to one Representative** for purposes of \u00a7 102(b) and Title III; and\n\n**(5)** **\"political party\"** means **an association of citizens that nominates one or more candidates for the office of Senator or Representative**, whether or not the association is recognized, qualified, or classified as a political party under the law of any State.\n\n**(c-1) A State's classification does not control.** The definition in subsection (c)(5) applies to every provision of this Act, and **a State may not, by classifying an association as something other than a political party, by withdrawing a recognition, or by imposing a condition on recognition, deny to that association any right this Act confers on a political party.** **A right conferred by this Act on a political party is conferred on every association within subsection (c)(5)**, and the conditions a State may impose are those stated in \u00a7 205(c), \u00a7 205(d) and \u00a7 303(a) and no others.\n\n**(d) Purpose.** This Act is to be construed to give the fullest effect to the election of Representatives in proportion to the support of the voters, as \u00a7 203 measures it."},{"anchor":"sec-505","heading":"\u00a7 505. Actions by aggrieved persons","part":"TITLE V \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) The right and the action.** A person aggrieved by a violation of any requirement of this Act **may bring a civil action** in a district court of the United States for declaratory and injunctive relief and for such other relief as the court finds appropriate, **and the court shall award a prevailing plaintiff reasonable attorney's fees, expert fees and costs.**\n\n**(a-1) Aggrieved.** A person is **aggrieved** where the person is subject to, or will be subject to, the plan, method or practice complained of, **or is denied information this Act entitles the person to receive.** An organization is aggrieved where a member is, **and need not identify the member by name in the pleading.** A candidate and a political party are aggrieved by a violation of Title II or Title III affecting an election in which they participate. **This subsection identifies the persons on whom this Act confers a right; it does not enlarge or restrict the judicial power.**\n\n**(a-2) Pleading.** A complaint states a claim where it alleges facts which, if proved, would establish the elements of the violation. **No heightened standard of pleading applies.**\n\n**(a-3) Abrogation.** A State, and an instrumentality or official of a State acting in an official capacity, **is subject to suit under this section in Federal or State court for the relief provided.** As to a claim arising under Title I, Title II or Title IV, this subsection rests upon the authority of Congress under Article I, section 4; as to a claim of denial or abridgement of the right to vote on account of race or color, it rests upon section 2 of the Fifteenth Amendment and section 5 of the Fourteenth Amendment, **and Congress states the abrogation expressly.**\n\n**(a-4) Severability by defendant.** The application of this section to a State or a State instrumentality or official is severable from its application to every other defendant.\n\n**(b) Expressly conferred.** The right and the action stated in subsection (a) are **conferred expressly by this section. No inference, implication or construction is required to establish either**, and no presumption against the creation of a private right or a private remedy applies.\n\n**(c) Three-judge court.** An action raising a claim under Title I or Title IV, **where brought in a district court of the United States**, **shall be heard by a court of three judges** convened under section 2284 of title 28, United States Code. This subsection does not limit subsection (g).\n\n**(d) No preconditions.** Neither exhaustion of any administrative remedy, nor notice to any officer, nor action or inaction by the Attorney General, is a condition of an action under this section. **A determination by the Attorney General not to act is not a bar, an adjudication, or evidence in any action.**\n\n**(e) The Attorney General.** The Attorney General may bring a civil action to enforce this Act and may intervene as of right in any action brought under it. **No action by the Attorney General is a prerequisite to any private action.**\n\n**(f) These and only these.** The limitations on an action under this section are those and only those expressly stated in this Act. **The omission of a limitation is a determination by Congress and not a gap.**\n\n**(f-1) When an action may be brought.** An action challenging a districting plan, or a method of election, **may be brought at any time while the plan or method is in force**, and the use of a plan or method at a previous election is not a bar. **No period of limitation applies to an action under this section**, and none may be supplied from any other statute.\n\n**(g) Concurrent jurisdiction; removal barred.** **(1)** An action under this section may be brought in a district court of the United States **or in any State court of competent jurisdiction.** **(2)** An action under this section brought in a State court **may not be removed to a district court of the United States.** **(3)** A State court shall not decline to hear an action under this section on the ground that it arises under Federal law, nor dismiss it on the ground that the plaintiff would lack standing in a court of the United States. **(4)** The abrogation stated in subsection (a-3) applies to an action brought in a State court."},{"anchor":"sec-506","heading":"\u00a7 506. Timing of relief","part":"TITLE V \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Proximity is one factor.** In an action under \u00a7 505 relating to an election to be held within the period stated in the SCHEDULE, the court shall consider the proximity of the election as one factor among others, **and proximity alone is not a ground for denying relief.**\n\n**(b) No blanket rule of repose.** No court shall adopt a categorical rule barring relief within a fixed period before an election."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"| Ref | Provision | Value | *(Reserved.)* |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S-1 | \u00a7 107(a) date by which a State shall have established conforming districts | 224 days after the transmission of the statement required by section 2a(a) of title 2, United States Code, or 136 days after the tabulations required by section 141(c) of title 13, United States Code, are first made available to the State in any format, whichever is later | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-2 | \u00a7 205(c) signatures required for ballot access by petition, as a number | 25 signatures, or the number in S-3 where it is less | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-3 | \u00a7 205(c) signatures required for ballot access by petition, as a share | 1 percent of the votes cast in the district at the preceding election for Representative | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-4 | \u00a7 205(d) maximum filing fee | $0 | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-5 | \u00a7 205(d-1)(1) period to notify a petitioner of rejected signatures | 14 days after the petition is delivered to the office the State designates to receive it | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-6 | \u00a7 205(d-1)(2) period to submit further signatures or to show a rejected signature valid | 7 days after the petitioner receives notice; notice sent electronically to an address the petitioner supplies is received when sent, and notice sent by mail is received on the third day after mailing | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-7 | \u00a7 206(b) period for publication of the count | for the list totals, the candidate totals, the numbers summed over the district, the quotients, the result of each lot and the order in which seats were awarded, the day the State determines the seats each list wins, or the last day State law fixes for certifying the result, whichever is earlier, and in every case before it certifies the result; for the votes cast in each precinct and every other matter \u00a7 206(b) requires to be published, 14 days after the State certifies the result; after a recount under \u00a7 206(b-1), for the matters first named, the day the recount determines the seats each list wins, and in every case before its result is certified, and for every other matter, 14 days after the recount's result is certified | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-8 | \u00a7 208(a) payment per registered voter | [___] | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-9 | \u00a7 506(a) interim-relief proximity period | 90 days before the first day any ballot may be cast | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-10 | \u00a7 204(e) period for a person written in to file an acceptance of a seat | 7 days after the person receives notice; notice sent electronically to an address the person has supplied to the State is received when sent, and notice sent by mail is received on the third day after mailing; a statement sent electronically to an address the chief State election official publishes for the purpose not later than the day the polls close is filed when sent, and a statement mailed is filed on the date of its postmark where it is received not later than the third day after the period ends, and for every purpose of \u00a7 204(e) and (e-1) other than filing the period ends on that third day | *(Reserved.)* |"}],"asOf":"2026-11-19"}}}};
